<figure><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/apple-home-collection-lineup-261013-cleaned-6c.jpeg?ssl=1" alt="A lineup of minimalist smart home devices: a black keypad, silver deadbolt lock, white security camera on a stand, gray thermostat displaying 68, and a tall black-and-silver video doorbell." /><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>On Tuesday as a part of its deluge of smart home announcements, Apple has launched something a bit different: <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/10/introducing-apple-home-collection-a-new-lineup-of-smart-home-accessories/">a series of products called Apple Home Collection</a> that are&#8230; not quite Apple products, but not <em>not</em> Apple products. Instead, they&#8217;re &#8220;designed in collaboration&#8221; with partners, in this case LG Electronics and Schneider Electric.</p>
<p>It&#8217;s hard to tell where one company ends and the other begins, but if you carry the weight of a brand like Apple, having Apple stand over the shoulder of some other company and sprinkle in some secret ingredients isn&#8217;t the worst approach if you don&#8217;t want to actually build and support those products yourself.</p>
<p>According to Apple, each accessory can be set up with one tap from an iPhone, and only requires the Apple Home app. (That describes a lot of products today, too—it&#8217;ll be interesting to see if Apple&#8217;s added some new tech just for these products or if they&#8217;re largely using standard techniques that other products in the market can offer.)</p>
<p>Now to the products, which will be available starting in December, and all of which share a &#8220;clean, minimal aesthetic and premium materials,&#8221; which would probably be your first clue that Apple was involved in their design.</p>
<figure><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/apple-home-collection-lg-indoor-cam-plug-in-261013-6c.jpeg?ssl=1" alt="Two views of a white LG webcam on a round base with a vertical post. On the left, the cylindrical camera body faces away with an LG logo on top; on the right, it is tilted forward showing a black lens." /><figcaption>LG&#8217;s tilt-down Indoor Cam.</figcaption></figure>
<p>LG is offering four cameras, all of which capture 2.5K video and connect directly with iCloud for video via the HomeKit Secure Video specification.<sup id="fnref-41881-cams"><a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/10/new-home-collection-banner-offers-apple-influenced-accessories/#fn-41881-cams" class="jetpack-footnote" title="Read footnote.">1</a></sup> There&#8217;s a video doorbell (with chime integrated with HomeView, HomePod, and HomePod mini), a combination camera and floodlight, a standard outdoor camera, and an indoor camera that can tilt down automatically to physically stop monitoring when a family member enters the home.</p>
<figure><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/apple-home-collection-lifestyle-01-261013-6c.jpeg?ssl=1" alt="A dark green paneled front door with frosted glass, fitted with a black keypad smart lock, a slim bronze doorbell camera and a bronze lever handle. A yellow brick wall, white column and leafy green shrub frame the entrance." /><figcaption>The LG smart lock and doorbell in situ.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Apple gets in the smart lock game with LG&#8217;s Smart Deadbolt Lock, which uses UWB for automatic unlocking when you approach the door, like the locks released earlier this year by <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/03/aqara-uwb-smart-lock-u400-review-beam-me-up/">Aqara</a> and <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/08/schlage-sense-pro-uwb-smart-lock-review-what-price-beauty/">Schlage</a>. It&#8217;s also got NFC support for non-UWB devices, a numeric keypad, and a hidden physical key slot.</p>
<p>And then there are the smart thermostats. There are two of these, from LG and Schneider Electric. The two thermostats support external temperature and humidity sensors as well as those built in to HomeView, HomePod, and HomePod mini. It&#8217;s unclear what differences the two products have, but Apple&#8217;s press release hints that there might be some different kinds of system compatibility, perhaps for the home heating and cooling systems themselves, though both are described as working &#8220;with a broad range of HVAC systems.&#8221;</p>
<p>Every one of the accessories supports Thread, which Apple has been building into its devices for a while now. While it doesn&#8217;t seem like there&#8217;s much here that hasn&#8217;t been done in other smart home products, Apple&#8217;s stamp of approval is important. If you don&#8217;t do a lot of research, even today you can end up buying a smart home product that has hidden gotchas. These devices will presumably be as standard and Apple-friendly as they come.</p>
<p>That said, other smart home products also seem to live up to everything Apple is describing with the Home Collection. Until these products ship, it will be hard to determine if there&#8217;s some special stuff Apple is doing with these products that&#8217;s unavailable to other products, but at a glance it doesn&#8217;t seem so.</p>
